  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 641 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nWe will continue to be in a northwest flow regime aloft for most of \nthe long term period. However, by Saturday, this pattern begins to \nbreak down as the upper trough to the north/northeast flattens and \nthe upper ridge over the Desert Southwest flattens as well and pushes\nfarther east into the ArkLaTex. A lingering surface boundary will \nalso persist over the Southeast into Thursday before what looks like \na reinforcing cold front swings through. By late week and into the \nfirst part of next weekend high pressure builds over the Appalachians\nand Ohio Valley. \n\nOverall, highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s with lows in the mid \n60s to lower 70s will be common through the period. In addition, the \nabove pattern will lead to daily chances of showers and storms from \nTuesday through Thursday at least. Expecting lower chances come \nFriday/Saturday. The best chances look to be during the afternoon and\nevening hours, during peak heating when storms can take advantage of\nthe greater thermodynamics in place. Ample instability in addition \nto bulk shear between 20-30 knots, 0-3km lapse rates between 7-8 deg \nC/km, along with DCAPE around or above 1000 J/kg, will warrant us \nkeeping an eye on the forecast for at least strong to severe \ndownburst potential Wednesday and Thursday. As has been the case \nrecently, the main hazards are likely to be damaging winds, frequent \nlightning, and perhaps some hail (but freezing levels may be too \nhigh). As for heavy rain and flooding concerns, this will need to be \nmonitored as well, since most PWATS are generally between 1.7-2.0 \ninches Tuesday through Thursday. These values are between the 75th \nand 90th percentiles when compared to BMX Sounding Climatology, which\nmeans that showers and storms will be efficient rainfall producers. \nStay weather aware especially if you work outside or have outdoor \nactivities and check back for forecast updates!",
